00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:39.870
So basically, very simply,
this product or service--

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:44.220
it moves data from a source
to what we call a data sync.

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:46.357
Now you could call
it a sink, a bathtub,

00:09:46.357 --> 00:09:47.940
a pool-- whatever
you want to call it.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:49.356
What it's going
to end up being in

00:09:49.356 --> 00:09:50.770
is a Google Cloud
Storage bucket.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:54.270
So you are moving data
from a cloud storage source

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:57.240
to a Google Cloud
storage bucket.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:59.550
It's really good
to use this product

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:03.660
when you want to synchronize
your data from source

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:05.449
to your Google Cloud
storage bucket.

00:10:05.449 --> 00:10:06.990
You expect things
to change, you want

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:12.420
to make sure the source
and the destination match.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:15.480
It also automates the
deletion of source data

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:16.980
once you move it or vise versa.

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:18.230
NICK ORLOVE: Oh, that's great.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:21.310
CHRIS TALBOTT: So you can,
for lack of a better word,

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:23.730
it essentially has
checksumming between the source

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.520
and the destination
of your data.

00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:30.210
And for those customers who
are already using Google Cloud

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:33.720
storage and, say, you want
to move from one region

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:37.350
to another region, or you want
to tier a large amount of data

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.030
