00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:50.730
So lots of marketers are
using BigQuery to analyze

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:52.350
what their customers are doing.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:56.265
And this tool is fully
managed data import service

00:10:56.265 --> 00:10:57.450
for Google BigQuery.

00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:00.499
So you see some of the
very popular ads, products,

00:11:00.499 --> 00:11:02.040
on the left-hand
side of the screen--

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:04.780
YouTube, AdWords, DoubleClick.

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:06.750
BigQuery can automate
the transfer of data

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.595
from those products
directly into BigQuery.

00:11:09.595 --> 00:11:10.470
NICK ORLOVE: Awesome.

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:11.100
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yes.

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:12.720
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah,
that's really cool.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.178
And what's nice is
that BigQuery is

00:11:14.178 --> 00:11:16.380
used for all sorts of
different data sets--

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:18.930
data types for analyzing
data, whether it's ad hoc,

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:21.450
batch analysis,
streaming, et cetera.

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:24.330
But it's not just
for the data that you

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:26.800
use through your marketing
data or what have you.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:31.160
So we have customers who import
their data with SaaS sources.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:33.010
For instance, if you
have marketing data,

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:36.240
you have CRM data,
maybe event-driven data,

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:39.930
or maybe you have databases--
so SQL data, whether it's

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:44.480
a MySQL, or PostgreS, SQL
Server, or even Oracle, finding

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:46.920
some way of basically
moving that data

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:50.370
so that you can perform lots of
analysis on multiple data sets,

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:54.030
combining the things together
to get one rational picture.

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:56.000
And even your existing
data warehouses--

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.710
so if you have Teradata,
Netezza, et cetera,

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:03.150
it's really a great source of
basically housing your data.

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:04.850
And I like how you
called it your sink,

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:06.120
your pool, et cetera.

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:09.390
That's definitely applicable,
because we meet customers

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:10.140
wherever they are.

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:12.120
And the benefit of
BigQuery is that we

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:14.320
scale to meet their demands.

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:17.350
So whether you have just
a couple gigs, a couple

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:20.220
terabytes, a couple petabytes,
dozens of petabytes,

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:23.100
we can definitely meet you
where you're at with BigQuery.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:25.800
And we have partners,
as you can see

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:29.440
on the screen, who are able
to help migrate that data.

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:35.160
So whether you are working with
a SaaS application or a storage

00:12:35.160 --> 00:12:37.300
application in
terms of databases,

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:38.940
we have a bunch of
partners who are

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:42.630
able to help migrate
that data efficiently

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:44.700
and move it into BigQuery
for further analysis.

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:48.120
CHRIS TALBOTT: That's great.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:49.190
NICK ORLOVE: So I guess--

00:12:49.190 --> 00:12:50.990
let me ask the question of--

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:54.140
so let's say that I
have a slow network,

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:57.440
and I want to migrate a
whole lot of data over.

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:58.360
What are my options?

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:00.318
CHRIS TALBOTT: Well, we
have a product for you.

00:13:00.318 --> 00:13:01.175
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:01.175 --> 00:13:03.242
So Transfer Appliance--
I said I wanted

00:13:03.242 --> 00:13:05.450
to take a little bit more
time to talk about Transfer

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:07.991
Appliance, because it's a brand
new product to the portfolio,

00:13:07.991 --> 00:13:08.870
essentially.

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:11.810
It's our rackable, high
capacity storage server

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:15.830
for shipping large amounts
of data to Google Cloud

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:17.990
rather than sending
it over the network.

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:19.880
So you can see on the
screen, right here

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:24.020
is the 100 terabyte raw
capacity version, which,

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:26.150
with compression,
you can achieve

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:29.980
potentially double that
amount of data on the device

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:32.000
and then send us 200 terabytes.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:37.550
We also have a [? 4U ?] version,
480 terabytes of raw capacity.

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:41.300
And this device could move
up to a petabyte of data

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:42.950
to the Cloud.

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:46.010
And what's amazing is
it does it much faster

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:47.780
than a typical
network would do it.

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:49.490
So as we watch this
screen build out,

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:52.430
we can see Transfer Appliance
could move a petabyte of data

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:54.920
potentially, if you're
really fast with the shipping

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.200
and making sure you get
it back to us in time--

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:58.700
in 43 days.

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:01.720
Compare that to what you could
do over a typical network.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:05.610
That's like 96% faster
to move your data.

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:07.095
So back to your
comment about CEOs

00:14:07.095 --> 00:14:08.720
want to move at the
speed of, you know,

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:10.704
digital transformation--
this is a product

00:14:10.704 --> 00:14:13.120
that could help you do that
and get your data to the Cloud

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:13.630
faster.

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:14.900
NICK ORLOVE: That's cool.

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:17.150
CHRIS TALBOTT: It's also
a really simple process,

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:21.090
similar to e-commerce
and online shopping.

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:25.260
So all you have to do is go
into the Google Cloud console.

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:28.710
You request a
Transfer Appliance.

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:32.390
We'll actually reach out
and work with you to size

00:14:32.390 --> 00:14:35.930
the right appliance
for your workload.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:38.270
A lot of folks who might
have small amounts of data

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:41.030
and perhaps Transfer
Appliance isn't necessarily

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:41.780
the best solution.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:44.720
We could probably do it with
gsutil or some networking

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.310
solutions.

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:49.250
Perhaps, say, it falls somewhere
in between that 100 terabytes

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:50.660
and 480 terabytes,
and they don't

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:53.280
know should I order four of
one and one of the other?

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:57.584
So we'll help you out with
that, with those considerations.

00:14:57.584 --> 00:14:59.000
Once you request
the appliance, we

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:04.580
ship it by regular shipping
carriers like FedEx and UPS.

00:15:04.580 --> 00:15:06.140
You receive the appliance.

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:08.360
You actually, if you
want to, can rack it

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:09.570
in your data center.

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:12.470
Which is one of the reasons
why we built transfer

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:13.970
appliance the way that we did.

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:17.150
Because we know,
one, lots of folks

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:19.070
have existing data centers.

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:20.720
And two, those
data centers don't

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:23.510
come with space
for various devices

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:25.055
to be littered around the floor.

00:15:25.055 --> 00:15:26.180
NICK ORLOVE: True.

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:27.140
CHRIS TALBOTT: And
three, if you're

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:28.580
moving a petabyte
of data, it's going

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:30.246
to have to live there
for a little while

00:15:30.246 --> 00:15:33.947
as it captures all of the
data that you want to move.

00:15:33.947 --> 00:15:36.280
So you can actually rack
Transfer Appliance in your data

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:38.600
center, connect
it to the network,

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:40.780
fill it up with up to
a petabyte of data,

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:42.530
if you buy the big one.

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:44.840
And then you ship the
appliance back to Google,

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:49.550
where we stage that in a Google
Cloud Storage bucket, where

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:51.290
the data then gets decrypted.

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:52.582
It's encrypted the entire time.

00:15:52.582 --> 00:15:54.123
NICK ORLOVE: Oh,
that's good to know.

00:15:54.123 --> 00:15:56.600
CHRIS TALBOTT: So your data
is secure as you transfer it.

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:59.630
And then all you do is, you log
back into Google Cloud Storage.

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:03.320
You supply the decryption keys.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:04.230
Decrypt that data.

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:07.310
And we'll have it available in
a Google Cloud Storage bucket

00:16:07.310 --> 00:16:09.800
and then can make that
data available to all

00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:12.525
of the services that you may
want to use from Google Cloud

00:16:12.525 --> 00:16:12.670
platform.

00:16:12.670 --> 00:16:13.420
NICK ORLOVE: Nice.

00:16:13.420 --> 00:16:15.710
And these are decryption
keys that I as a customer

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:17.570
set myself, and
so Google doesn't

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:20.210
know what data I'm sending
or has any access to any

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:20.860
of that stuff.

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:21.693
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yeah.

00:16:23.746 --> 00:16:24.870
That's a really good point.

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:30.020
Because as the data transfers
via FedEx across the country

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:32.000
and wherever you
may be sending it,

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:33.540
we want that data
to be protected.

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:36.030
So it's going to be
encrypted the entire time.

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:38.720
And once the data
transfer is complete,

00:16:38.720 --> 00:16:42.110
the appliances are raised
per the standards, which

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:43.770
[? I ?] feel confident with.

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:45.415
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:16:45.415 --> 00:16:47.540
CHRIS TALBOTT: So I mentioned
this product recently

