00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:14.220
some sort of file storage.

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:17.510
So I just want to make it really
easy for you to drag and drop

00:17:17.510 --> 00:17:20.839
your files or rsync those
files from where they are today

00:17:20.839 --> 00:17:23.359
into a Transfer Appliance.

00:17:23.359 --> 00:17:26.090
We released an in
console request process,

00:17:26.090 --> 00:17:29.480
so you can see and
request transfer appliance

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:32.310
in the same console that you're
using Google Cloud Storage

00:17:32.310 --> 00:17:33.986
and all the other services.

00:17:33.986 --> 00:17:35.360
And then, an end
to end integrity

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:37.370
check-- so this goes back
to one of those challenges

00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:39.620
that I mentioned at the
beginning of the presentation.

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:42.560
You want to ensure that the
bits in the source and the bits

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:44.930
in the destination
are exactly the same.

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:48.810
Because you want any application
that's referencing them,

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:52.729
any analyst who wants to analyze
that data to ensure they're

00:17:52.729 --> 00:17:53.770
looking at the same data.

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:55.502
So we have an end to
end integrity check

00:17:55.502 --> 00:17:57.460
to make sure that you're
confident in doing so.

00:17:57.460 --> 00:18:00.100
NICK ORLOVE: Yep.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:01.620
CHRIS TALBOTT: So
Transfer Appliance

