00:21:47.558 --> 00:21:48.950
all these amazing images.

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:51.170
Obviously, that accounts
for a lot of data

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:53.040
that they were
collecting on premises.

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:56.160
So they use Transfer Appliance
to move that data to the Cloud.

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:00.810
Beatport has a really
interesting use case.

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:04.940
And they want to be able to
serve their library of music

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:08.200
content as fast as
possible to listeners.

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:10.191
So they used Transfer
Appliance to move

00:22:10.191 --> 00:22:11.690
the entire digital
library that they

00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:13.010
have into Google Cloud Storage.

00:22:13.010 --> 00:22:15.890
Because they knew that Google
Cloud Storage performance,

00:22:15.890 --> 00:22:18.860
in particular with content
serving, is the best out there.

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:23.812
And then finally, I mentioned
that interesting use

00:22:23.812 --> 00:22:25.520
case of collecting
research from vendors.

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:29.249
So this is actually a Google
X company called Makani.

00:22:29.249 --> 00:22:31.040
And what they do, you
can see in the image,

00:22:31.040 --> 00:22:34.790
is they fly wind kites
really high up in the air.

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:37.250
Because it turns out
the wind blows harder

00:22:37.250 --> 00:22:39.200
if you go up a few
hundred meters, right?

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:41.510
So they can generate
energy faster

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:44.150
with turbines that are a
hundred meters into the air

00:22:44.150 --> 00:22:47.765
than you could in something
that's just a typical windmill.

00:22:47.765 --> 00:22:48.515
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

00:22:48.515 --> 00:22:48.920
Wow.

00:22:48.920 --> 00:22:51.260
That's got to be an interesting
challenge to deal with.

00:22:51.260 --> 00:22:52.610
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yeah.

00:22:52.610 --> 00:22:55.070
But it's really important that
they pick the right spots.

00:22:55.070 --> 00:22:59.120
Because you need to pick an area
that weather-wise and wind-wise

00:22:59.120 --> 00:22:59.810
is appropriate.

00:22:59.810 --> 00:23:01.950
So they use Transfer
Appliance to collect data

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:05.060
from research vendors,
bring it into Google Cloud.

00:23:05.060 --> 00:23:06.950
and then analyze and
run some models on that

00:23:06.950 --> 00:23:11.210
to pick where they're going
to tether their wind kites.

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:15.170
Now regardless of what your
use case is, what kind of data

00:23:15.170 --> 00:23:18.580
you're moving, it's going to
end up in Google Cloud Storage.

00:23:18.580 --> 00:23:21.710
So let's take a second
to introduce or remind

00:23:21.710 --> 00:23:23.660
folks of what Google
Cloud Storage is.

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:25.940
It's our object
storage solution.

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:28.520
It's one product, which
is very important.

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:30.830
Because that's differentiated
in the marketplace.

00:23:30.830 --> 00:23:33.200
It essentially comes
in four flavors,

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:37.100
based on the availability
or redundancy

00:23:37.100 --> 00:23:40.050
that you need for your
particular workload.

00:23:40.050 --> 00:23:41.840
So from left to
right on this screen,

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:45.800
you have multi-regional, which
means geo redundant storage,

00:23:45.800 --> 00:23:49.557
regional, which means in
one particular region where

00:23:49.557 --> 00:23:50.390
your data is stored.

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:53.600
Nearline and Coldline, which are
essentially cold and archival

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:54.710
storage options.

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:57.350
And the price follows
the availability

00:23:57.350 --> 00:23:59.004
from left to right
on this screen.

00:23:59.004 --> 00:24:01.170
NICK ORLOVE: And Chris,
I've got a question for you.

00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:03.350
And just as a reminder
to you, our audience,

00:24:03.350 --> 00:24:04.980
there's live Q&amp;A at the bottom.

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:06.130
So make sure you type
in these questions.

00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:08.480
And we'll answer some
questions at the end.

00:24:08.480 --> 00:24:11.100
But I frequently hear this
question from customers

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:14.180
of-- all right, so there's
four different tiers of GCS--

00:24:14.180 --> 00:24:16.460
the Multiregional, Regional,
Nearline, and Coldline.

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:19.550
That kind of implies that,
as you tier things down,

00:24:19.550 --> 00:24:21.500
you're getting a
degraded performance.

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:23.330
I mean, how does
performance work

00:24:23.330 --> 00:24:25.079
in terms of these
different four flavors?

00:24:25.079 --> 00:24:25.745
CHRIS TALBOTT: .

00:24:25.745 --> 00:24:28.265
Yeah that's a really
good question.

00:24:28.265 --> 00:24:30.140
And it's a really awesome
answer from Google,

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:31.800
because it doesn't change.

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:34.880
So the user experience
of requesting

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:37.880
a object in Multiregional
or requesting

00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:40.370
an object in Coldline
will be exactly the same.

00:24:40.370 --> 00:24:43.160
It will come back in
milliseconds of latency--

00:24:43.160 --> 00:24:46.220
because essentially, a
single API across these.

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:49.340
We just allow you the
option to tier that storage

00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:50.600
based on costs.

00:24:50.600 --> 00:24:52.970
Or stuff that's not going to
get accessed as frequently.

00:24:52.970 --> 00:24:54.100
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah, OK.

00:24:54.100 --> 00:24:56.500
That makes sense.

00:24:56.500 --> 00:24:57.130
Cool.

00:24:57.130 --> 00:25:01.150
So I have, obviously, a lot of
customers who are using GCS.

00:25:01.150 --> 00:25:03.160
And so I think it's
valuable to talk

00:25:03.160 --> 00:25:06.422
about the different reasons
people typically use GCS.

00:25:06.422 --> 00:25:08.380
And those generally fall
into the three buckets

00:25:08.380 --> 00:25:09.650
that you see on the screen.

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:11.660
So first being content
storage and delivery--

00:25:11.660 --> 00:25:15.970
so GCS is essentially
limitless object storage.

00:25:15.970 --> 00:25:20.050
So whether you're storing videos
like Vimeo, music like Spotify,

00:25:20.050 --> 00:25:24.410
websites, multimedia, maybe
your mobile web applications,

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:27.220
it is a fantastic place
for storing your content

00:25:27.220 --> 00:25:29.980
for the delivery through--
whether it be through CDNs,

00:25:29.980 --> 00:25:32.920
or, like you said, through the
incredible latency that GCS

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:34.630
naturally provides.

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:37.060
The second big category
is around data analytics

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:38.470
and general compute.

00:25:38.470 --> 00:25:42.100
So if anyone is familiar with
Hadoop, for instance, or GCE,

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:46.596
you can leverage Dataproc to
store all of your back end

00:25:46.596 --> 00:25:48.970
analytics data that you're
going to crawl through, create

00:25:48.970 --> 00:25:51.900
Hadoop clusters, destroy
Hadoop clusters, et cetera.

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:53.980
And it really helps
in terms of being

00:25:53.980 --> 00:25:58.000
able to scale storage and
compute independently.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:25:59.650
And so we have a
bunch of customers,

00:25:59.650 --> 00:26:01.540
for instance, the
Broad Institute,

00:26:01.540 --> 00:26:05.170
who uses GCS for doing
some genomics data.

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:07.810
Because obviously, those
genomic data sets are massive.

00:26:07.810 --> 00:26:08.920
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yes.

00:26:08.920 --> 00:26:11.050
NICK ORLOVE: Or Philips
is another example

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:15.040
of being able to collect
all of that IT sensor data

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:18.160
from everyone really
across the globe

00:26:18.160 --> 00:26:20.800
and being able to surface
it up through the Philips

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:25.690
app, et cetera, being able
to drive a lot more insights

00:26:25.690 --> 00:26:27.280
and capabilities
to their customers

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:29.330
than what would have
normally been possible.

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:30.310
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yeah.

00:26:30.310 --> 00:26:31.976
NICK ORLOVE: And then
the final category

00:26:31.976 --> 00:26:35.890
being kind of the general
backup, archival, and storage

00:26:35.890 --> 00:26:37.430
repository for your data.

00:26:37.430 --> 00:26:41.740
So if you are interested in,
for instance, a hybrid cloud

00:26:41.740 --> 00:26:43.510
approach or multi
cloud approach,

00:26:43.510 --> 00:26:45.590
being able to store your
data in multiple places,

00:26:45.590 --> 00:26:47.799
whether that be through a
backup, through an archive.

00:26:47.799 --> 00:26:49.923
And what's nice, is that
we work with a whole bunch

00:26:49.923 --> 00:26:52.280
of different partners, for
instance, [INAUDIBLE] NetApp,

00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:56.170
[INAUDIBLE],, Veritas, et cetera,
where you can essentially

00:26:56.170 --> 00:27:00.470
leverage your existing solution
on prem or in the Cloud.

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:04.180
And then configure as a
backup repository GCS,

00:27:04.180 --> 00:27:07.450
so that you can store
your backup data in GCS

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:08.260
more efficiently.

00:27:08.260 --> 00:27:10.030
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yeah, most
of those most common backup

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:12.195
providers you can just
choose GCS as a drop down.

00:27:12.195 --> 00:27:13.570
NICK ORLOVE:
Exactly, yeah, yeah.

00:27:13.570 --> 00:27:14.600
I've done it before.

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:16.370
Yep.

00:27:16.370 --> 00:27:18.700
CHRIS TALBOTT: So I wanted
to kind of double click

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:19.480
on one point.

00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:23.529
I mentioned one product
per GCS, which is really

00:27:23.529 --> 00:27:24.820
important in the storage world.

00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:26.800
Because when it
comes to storage,

00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:29.120
we want a lot of rich features.

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:31.990
You want to have some of
your data being geo redundant

00:27:31.990 --> 00:27:33.580
in case of a disaster.

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:37.180
You probably want some of your
data to be pushed to the edge,

00:27:37.180 --> 00:27:39.740
close to your users as
possible so that the experience

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:42.070
is really, really fast.

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:45.307
You want to have some of
your data in cold storage,

00:27:45.307 --> 00:27:47.890
so that you don't have to pay
as much because it's not getting

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:50.690
used for the next three years.

00:27:50.690 --> 00:27:52.780
So with Google
Cloud Storage, you

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:55.920
get all of those features
in one single product,

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:57.250
in a single API.

00:27:57.250 --> 00:27:59.740
Where if you went out in
the marketplace, typically

00:27:59.740 --> 00:28:02.920
you're going to have to purchase
multiple products in order

00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:06.470
to achieve the same
amount of functionality.

00:28:06.470 --> 00:28:09.275
So I kind of wanted to prove
that really quick in a demo.

00:28:09.275 --> 00:28:10.025
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

00:28:10.025 --> 00:28:10.290
Let's do it.

00:28:10.290 --> 00:28:12.498
CHRIS TALBOTT: So we're
going to talk, in particular,

00:28:12.498 --> 00:28:14.950
about edge caching.

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:19.000
So obviously, lots of customers
serve content to users.

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:22.270
And they serve content to those
users throughout the globe.

00:28:22.270 --> 00:28:24.520
So what I'm going
to essentially mimic

00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:28.600
