00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:28.570
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

00:29:28.570 --> 00:29:30.410
CHRIS TALBOTT: So
305 milliseconds--

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:32.500
so I have a Mumbai
bucket, thankfully.

00:29:32.500 --> 00:29:33.340
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:34.840
CHRIS TALBOTT: And
we'll take a look

00:29:34.840 --> 00:29:37.430
at the data that's in
this Mumbai bucket.

00:29:37.430 --> 00:29:39.290
So for those who
aren't familiar,

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:41.690
object storage works--
essentially, you have buckets.

00:29:41.690 --> 00:29:44.106
And you put data into that,
