00:31:03.940 --> 00:31:08.800
and it wants to push that image
as close to me as possible,

00:31:08.800 --> 00:31:11.950
so probably in an edge
location somewhere here

00:31:11.950 --> 00:31:13.324
in Mountain View.

00:31:13.324 --> 00:31:13.990
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:31:13.990 --> 00:31:14.490
Wow.

00:31:14.490 --> 00:31:15.890
That's cool.

00:31:15.890 --> 00:31:19.940
CHRIS TALBOTT: So let's get
back over to the slides.

00:31:19.940 --> 00:31:22.560
And let's talk about
the first option

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:25.315
that we introduced, gsutil and
some of the performance tricks

00:31:25.315 --> 00:31:26.190
associated with that.

00:31:26.190 --> 00:31:27.610
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah, definitely.

00:31:27.610 --> 00:31:30.600
So I frequently hear this
comment from customers of--

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:32.130
I have a lot of
data to transfer.

00:31:32.130 --> 00:31:33.600
I've decided on gsutil.

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:36.390
Are there some ways
of optimizing gsutil

00:31:36.390 --> 00:31:38.214
so that I can migrate
the data easier?

00:31:38.214 --> 00:31:39.130
And the answer is yes.

00:31:39.130 --> 00:31:40.754
So there's a couple
of slides that I'll

00:31:40.754 --> 00:31:44.530
go through, kind of tips
and tricks on how to do it.

00:31:44.530 --> 00:31:47.970
First thing to be mindful of is
location, location, location.

00:31:47.970 --> 00:31:51.000
So just like you showed,
if you have all your data

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:53.970
stored in the US, and you create
a storage bucket in Mumbai,

00:31:53.970 --> 00:31:56.070
and then you replicate
all your data to Mumbai,

00:31:56.070 --> 00:31:59.500
probably not going to be the
most efficient way of doing it.

00:31:59.500 --> 00:32:02.265
In fact, we have this
site, Cloud.Google.com

00:32:02.265 --> 00:32:06.690
/about/locations, where we
have currently over 16 regions.

00:32:06.690 --> 00:32:11.670
I think it's 49 zones in
35 different countries

00:32:11.670 --> 00:32:13.410
and over 100 points of presence.

00:32:13.410 --> 00:32:17.880
So definitely find the
region that's closest to you.

00:32:17.880 --> 00:32:19.260
In fact, later
this summer, we're

00:32:19.260 --> 00:32:20.710
opening up a region
in Los Angeles,

00:32:20.710 --> 00:32:21.709
which is pretty awesome.

00:32:21.709 --> 00:32:25.050
So make sure you stay up to
date with the regions that

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:27.050
are available.

00:32:27.050 --> 00:32:30.364
Secondly, if you're uploading
a lot of small files,

00:32:30.364 --> 00:32:31.280
you may not know this.

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:37.990
But every object storage out
there has some sort of latency

00:32:37.990 --> 00:32:42.550
or some sort of small overhead
that happens when you actually

00:32:42.550 --> 00:32:47.080
push a file to the ending
destination or the ending

00:32:47.080 --> 00:32:48.140
bucket.

00:32:48.140 --> 00:32:50.330
So being able to
push, let's say,

00:32:50.330 --> 00:32:55.300
500 small files of
125 kilobytes in size

00:32:55.300 --> 00:32:57.280
actually gets associated
with a large amount

00:32:57.280 --> 00:32:58.970
of unnecessary overhead.

00:32:58.970 --> 00:33:03.190
So if you consider gsutil's
multi-threaded option,

00:33:03.190 --> 00:33:07.240
the gsutil-m option,
you can actually

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:13.480
multithread all of these smaller
files into one big push to GCS.

00:33:13.480 --> 00:33:16.910
And as you can see on
the graph on the left,

00:33:16.910 --> 00:33:19.870
we did a test where
we actually saw over

00:33:19.870 --> 00:33:21.880
four times the
amount of throughput

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:23.371
just by using this -m option.

00:33:23.371 --> 00:33:25.120
So definitely something
to consider if you

00:33:25.120 --> 00:33:27.330
have a bunch of small files.

00:33:27.330 --> 00:33:30.130
Now conversely, if you have,
maybe, some really large files,

00:33:30.130 --> 00:33:32.520
so you're doing some video
uploads, some multimedia

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:34.950
uploads, maybe some
really large log files

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:37.320
or something like that,
there's another option

00:33:37.320 --> 00:33:40.691
of actually breaking one large
object into multiple smaller

00:33:40.691 --> 00:33:41.190
objects.

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:43.680
And it's called composite
parallel uploads.

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:46.770
So we have this,
another gsutil option,

00:33:46.770 --> 00:33:49.740
where essentially-- when you
perform an upload of, let's

00:33:49.740 --> 00:33:50.910
say, one massive file.

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:54.180
So on the left, you can
see a 500 megabyte file.

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:57.720
We actually split that
up into multiple chunks,

00:33:57.720 --> 00:34:00.240
upload those in
parallel, and then,

00:34:00.240 --> 00:34:04.890
once they all arrive
in GCS, GCS actually

00:34:04.890 --> 00:34:06.685
combines them together.

00:34:06.685 --> 00:34:09.060
And then performs a checksum
to make sure that everything

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:10.752
got compiled successfully.

00:34:10.752 --> 00:34:12.460
And as you can see on
the left-hand side,

00:34:12.460 --> 00:34:16.402
we see quite a bit of
performance improvement

00:34:16.402 --> 00:34:18.610
by using this option, compared
to the default option.

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:19.139
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yes.

00:34:19.139 --> 00:34:20.710
NICK ORLOVE: Now one thing
to be aware of, though,

00:34:20.710 --> 00:34:22.949
is that you should not be
using this for Nearline

00:34:22.949 --> 00:34:24.270
or Coldline storage.

00:34:24.270 --> 00:34:26.730
The reason why,
pretty simple, is

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:30.179
that when you upload all
these small little chunks,

00:34:30.179 --> 00:34:32.469
and then it combines those
chunks into one big chunk,

00:34:32.469 --> 00:34:35.250
it actually deletes
those individual chunks.

00:34:35.250 --> 00:34:37.739
And with Nearline
and Coldline storage,

00:34:37.739 --> 00:34:42.389
because these are longer
term storage mediums,

00:34:42.389 --> 00:34:44.940
your objects are meant to
stay for 30 days, 90 days,

00:34:44.940 --> 00:34:46.590
depending on the storage class.

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:49.860
So if you're performing a
composite parallel upload,

00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:51.900
you're actually, behind
the scenes, deleting

00:34:51.900 --> 00:34:54.120
each of those individual
chunks as they get

00:34:54.120 --> 00:34:55.870
combined into one big object.

00:34:55.870 --> 00:34:58.360
And so you'll get
charged, essentially,

00:34:58.360 --> 00:35:00.880
for those individual chunks
that have to be deleted.

00:35:00.880 --> 00:35:04.270
So makes sense-- just
something to be aware of.

00:35:04.270 --> 00:35:06.490
And another thing
to keep in mind

00:35:06.490 --> 00:35:11.060
is you have to be considerate
of sequential files.

00:35:11.060 --> 00:35:13.090
So if you're uploading,
for instance,

00:35:13.090 --> 00:35:16.270
log files or sensor
data that uses

00:35:16.270 --> 00:35:19.401
a traditional or standard kind
of naming convention, what

00:35:19.401 --> 00:35:21.400
happens under the covers
is that you're actually

00:35:21.400 --> 00:35:28.540
uploading the sequential files
to, maybe, the same back end--

00:35:28.540 --> 00:35:30.230
excuse me-- servers within GCS.

00:35:30.230 --> 00:35:32.740
So you can actually
be throttling yourself

00:35:32.740 --> 00:35:34.420
by just your naming
standard alone.

00:35:34.420 --> 00:35:36.910
So something to keep
in mind is, maybe,

00:35:36.910 --> 00:35:39.910
use a hash in front
of the file names,

00:35:39.910 --> 00:35:42.830
or maybe rename your file
folders, stuff like that.

00:35:42.830 --> 00:35:45.370
As you can see from the
screen shot on the bottom,

00:35:45.370 --> 00:35:48.940
we saw a 2x improvement
by simply renaming files,

00:35:48.940 --> 00:35:52.460
so another thing
to keep in mind.

00:35:52.460 --> 00:35:55.940
And then lastly, obviously
moving your data is important.

00:35:55.940 --> 00:35:58.600
But what if you have
an entire application

00:35:58.600 --> 00:35:59.860
that you want to move?

00:35:59.860 --> 00:36:04.660
You want to lift and shift
Application A from your data

00:36:04.660 --> 00:36:07.450
center to GCP.

00:36:07.450 --> 00:36:09.670
And you want it to remain
exactly like Application A.

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:10.030
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yes.

00:36:10.030 --> 00:36:10.720
NICK ORLOVE: Well,
fortunately, we

00:36:10.720 --> 00:36:13.390
have a whole bunch of
partners that we work with--

00:36:13.390 --> 00:36:17.870
for instance, [INAUDIBLE],,
who can do that for you.

00:36:17.870 --> 00:36:21.280
So if you have a Windows
Server, you have a Linux server,

00:36:21.280 --> 00:36:23.770
maybe have a VM environment,
you can perform that lift

00:36:23.770 --> 00:36:25.230
and shift quite easily.

00:36:25.230 --> 00:36:25.730
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yeah, awesome.

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:26.646
NICK ORLOVE: So, yeah.

00:36:26.646 --> 00:36:28.450
It's fantastic.

00:36:28.450 --> 00:36:32.230
So with that, we're going
to stay tuned for live Q&amp;A.

00:36:32.230 --> 00:36:33.972
So we'll be back in
less than a minute.

00:36:33.972 --> 00:36:35.305
And we'll answer your questions.

00:36:35.305 --> 00:36:36.138
CHRIS TALBOTT: Cool.

00:38:53.475 --> 00:38:54.600
NICK ORLOVE: Hey, everyone.

00:38:54.600 --> 00:38:55.497
Welcome back.

00:38:55.497 --> 00:38:57.080
So thank you, everyone,
for writing up

00:38:57.080 --> 00:38:58.220
a whole bunch of questions.

00:38:58.220 --> 00:38:59.780
We have some good ones.

00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:02.660
So Chris, I guess I'll ask
you the first question.

00:39:02.660 --> 00:39:05.480
When should you use gsutil
versus the storage transfer

00:39:05.480 --> 00:39:06.050
service?

00:39:06.050 --> 00:39:08.270
CHRIS TALBOTT: Great question--
cheaper, simple answer.

00:39:08.270 --> 00:39:08.936
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:39:08.936 --> 00:39:10.820
CHRIS TALBOTT: So when
you're moving data

00:39:10.820 --> 00:39:14.485
from on premises into
the Cloud and you

00:39:14.485 --> 00:39:15.860
don't have the
challenges that we

00:39:15.860 --> 00:39:18.320
talked about which would lead
you to Transfer Appliance,

00:39:18.320 --> 00:39:19.244
use gsutil.

00:39:19.244 --> 00:39:19.910
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:39:19.910 --> 00:39:22.140
CHRIS TALBOTT: Because
most of our developers,

00:39:22.140 --> 00:39:24.200
especially, use gsutil
to move their data.

00:39:24.200 --> 00:39:26.960
If you want to go from one
cloud storage location,

00:39:26.960 --> 00:39:29.660
whether that be in another
cloud storage provider or us,

00:39:29.660 --> 00:39:32.564
to a Google Cloud Storage
use transfer service.

00:39:32.564 --> 00:39:33.230
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:39:33.230 --> 00:39:34.800
Pretty clear cut, cool.

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:37.220
Well, I guess onto
the second question--

00:39:37.220 --> 00:39:40.820
how can I get the status or
other information on transfers?

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:42.780
Can this information
be visualized?

00:39:42.780 --> 00:39:46.790
CHRIS TALBOTT: So status
updates and further information

00:39:46.790 --> 00:39:48.610
on the progress
of your transfer--

00:39:48.610 --> 00:39:50.860
that's something that we're
thinking about integrating

00:39:50.860 --> 00:39:52.190
into the console in the future.

00:39:52.190 --> 00:39:52.994
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:39:52.994 --> 00:39:54.660
CHRIS TALBOTT: We
have standard tracking

00:39:54.660 --> 00:40:00.050
associated with the Appliance
through the shipping vendor.

00:40:00.050 --> 00:40:03.642
And that information, it cannot
be visualized today, obviously.

00:40:03.642 --> 00:40:05.330
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:40:05.330 --> 00:40:06.830
I guess I'll take the third one.

00:40:06.830 --> 00:40:08.960
Would you recommend
the Transfer Appliance

00:40:08.960 --> 00:40:12.350
to migrate on prem VMware
VMs to Google Cloud?

00:40:12.350 --> 00:40:15.440
So the Transfer Appliance
is more for data

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:17.390
itself, rather
than applications.

00:40:17.390 --> 00:40:20.210
So we actually have a
great solution specifically

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:22.810
for VMware called Velostrata.

00:40:22.810 --> 00:40:25.160
Velostrata was actually a
company that Google acquired,

00:40:25.160 --> 00:40:27.780
I think it was about a month
ago, maybe two months ago.

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:31.690
But it's really easy to,
basically, migrate your VMware

00:40:31.690 --> 00:40:34.950
VMs by essentially
installing little agents,

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:36.410
I think on vCenter.

00:40:36.410 --> 00:40:40.340
And then you have some
new options in VMware

00:40:40.340 --> 00:40:43.580
to essentially create the
destination where you're

00:40:43.580 --> 00:40:46.070
going to replicate your VM to.

00:40:46.070 --> 00:40:48.560
You'll, in the background,
synchronize the data.

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:50.180
And then we make it
very easy for you

00:40:50.180 --> 00:40:53.270
to perform that cut over
migration once you have all

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:55.460
the networking, the
firewalls, all the kind

00:40:55.460 --> 00:40:58.370
of ancillary pieces set up.

00:40:58.370 --> 00:41:00.830
Make it really easy for you.

00:41:00.830 --> 00:41:02.720
And by the way, the
Velostrata solution

00:41:02.720 --> 00:41:06.290
is totally free for performing
migrations into GCP.

00:41:06.290 --> 00:41:08.270
I mean, obviously,
there's GCP costs

00:41:08.270 --> 00:41:10.820
of running your VM on GCP.

00:41:10.820 --> 00:41:14.260
But the actual act of
migration through Velostrata

00:41:14.260 --> 00:41:14.960
is totally free.

00:41:14.960 --> 00:41:16.084
So definitely check it out.

00:41:16.084 --> 00:41:18.110
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yes.

00:41:18.110 --> 00:41:20.540
NICK ORLOVE: I guess I'll
take number four, as well.

00:41:20.540 --> 00:41:23.780
Rather than uploading my data
onto the Transfer Appliance,

00:41:23.780 --> 00:41:26.640
can I just ship you a
copy of my storage array?

00:41:26.640 --> 00:41:27.920
This is interesting.

00:41:27.920 --> 00:41:29.795
Because I used to be in
the storage industry,

00:41:29.795 --> 00:41:32.000
so I'm very familiar
with the storage arrays.

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:35.900
So the answer is,
unfortunately, no.

00:41:35.900 --> 00:41:38.420
You can't just ship us
your EMC or your NetApp

00:41:38.420 --> 00:41:40.030
or what have you--
storage array.

00:41:40.030 --> 00:41:41.600
At least, you can't ship us it.

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:44.330
But we do work with partners
like Iron Mountain, where

00:41:44.330 --> 00:41:47.480
you can ship your
storage to our partners.

00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:50.990
And they have incredibly fast
backbone network connection

00:41:50.990 --> 00:41:54.730
to GCP, and they can suck
all the data off your storage

00:41:54.730 --> 00:41:56.630
array, and then, put
it right into GCS.

00:41:56.630 --> 00:41:58.124
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yes.

00:41:58.124 --> 00:41:59.540
NICK ORLOVE: And
I guess I'll have

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:01.640
you take the last question.

00:42:01.640 --> 00:42:03.560
What if I have data
that changes frequently?

00:42:03.560 --> 00:42:05.241
Can I still use the
Transfer Appliance?

00:42:05.241 --> 00:42:06.740
CHRIS TALBOTT: Yeah,
you can use it.

00:42:06.740 --> 00:42:08.781
But probably not in the
way that you're thinking.

00:42:08.781 --> 00:42:14.110
So a great use case would be to
use Transfer Appliance to seed

00:42:14.110 --> 00:42:16.610
whatever that workload is that's
going to change frequently.

00:42:16.610 --> 00:42:18.714
So let's take backup
as an example.

00:42:18.714 --> 00:42:20.630
You could take all of
your historical backups,

00:42:20.630 --> 00:42:22.680
move them with
Transfer Appliance,

00:42:22.680 --> 00:42:26.114
and then incrementally back up
over the network from there.

00:42:26.114 --> 00:42:26.780
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:42:26.780 --> 00:42:26.990
CHRIS TALBOTT: Right?

00:42:26.990 --> 00:42:28.550
So if Transfer
Appliance can actually

00:42:28.550 --> 00:42:30.800
accelerate the process
of moving your data,

00:42:30.800 --> 00:42:32.720
even if it's going to change.

00:42:32.720 --> 00:42:34.010
NICK ORLOVE: OK, cool.

